<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Johannes Kepler</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1571">1571</a>–1630</span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Johannes Kepler was a German astronomer and mathematician, born in the imperial city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weil-der-stadt">Weil der Stadt</a>, who discovered the three laws of planetary motion. He served as imperial mathematician in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> and published the Rudolphine Tables in 1627. In 1628 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albrecht-von-wallenstein">Albrecht von Wallenstein</a> brought him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagan">Żagań</a> in Silesia, where he set up a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> press; he died in Regensburg two years later.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/johannes-kepler.jpg" alt="Johannes Kepler" width="320" height="460" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: August Köhler [1] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JKepler.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Rudolf II, Prague drew artists, alchemists and the astronomers Tycho Brahe and <b>Johannes Kepler</b>, and the Defenestration of Prague of 1618 set off the Thirty Years War, which reshaped Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bohemia">Kingdom of Bohemia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Tycho Brahe’s observatory of Uraniborg on the island of Hven, granted to him by Frederick II in 1576, produced the observations from which <b>Johannes Kepler</b> derived his laws of planetary motion.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-denmark">Kingdom of Denmark · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The work of Nicolaus Copernicus, Galileo Galilei, <b>Johannes Kepler</b> and René Descartes had overturned the ancient picture of the cosmos, and learned societies such as the Royal Society of London, founded in 1660, and the Paris Academy of Sciences, founded in 1666, organised experiment and the exchange of…</blockquote><a href="/movement/enlightenment">Enlightenment · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Tycho’s records passed to his assistant <b>Johannes Kepler</b>, who showed in his New Astronomy (1609) that Mars moves not in a circle but in an ellipse with the Sun at one focus, and that the line joining it to the Sun sweeps out equal areas in equal times;…</blockquote><a href="/movement/scientific-revolution">Scientific Revolution · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>It later returned to the Bohemian crown and in 1628 was granted to the general Albrecht von Wallenstein, who brought <b>Johannes Kepler</b> there.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-zagan">Duchy of Żagań</a></li><li><blockquote>The astronomer <b>Johannes Kepler</b> was born there in 1571.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-weil-der-stadt">Free Imperial City of Weil der Stadt</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/weil-der-stadt">Weil der Stadt</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in Regensburg, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.75,8.87&amp;year=1609" aria-label="Weil der Stadt on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4755 461 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Johannes Kepler"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5205 731h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5205 731h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.75,8.87&amp;year=1609">What was here in 1609? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;The die is cast; the book is written, to be read either now or by posterity, I care not which. It may well wait a century for a reader, as God has waited six thousand years for an observer.&#8221;</p><footer>Harmonices Mundi, book 5 (1619) &#183; tr. David Brewster, 1841 &#183; quoted in David Brewster, The Martyrs of Science &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/25992/pg25992-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I will triumph over mankind by the honest confession, that I have stolen the golden vases of the Egyptians, to build up a tabernacle for my God, far away from the confines of Egypt.&#8221;</p><footer>Harmonices Mundi, book 5 (1619) &#183; tr. David Brewster, 1841 &#183; quoted in David Brewster, The Martyrs of Science &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/25992/pg25992-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;O telescope, instrument of much knowledge, more precious than any sceptre! Is not he who holds thee in his hand made king and lord of the works of God?&#8221;</p><footer>Dioptrice, preface (1611) &#183; tr. Edward Stafford Carlos, 1880 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/46036/pg46036-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>2 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;What have plane figures to do with solid orbits? Solid bodies ought to be used for solid orbits.&#8221;</p><footer>On his search for the order of the planets, c. 1595 (Brewster, The Martyrs of Science) &#183; tr. David Brewster, 1841 &#183; as David Brewster quotes him &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/25992/pg25992-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…she seemed favourably inclined to the proposal; it is certain that she took time to consider it, but at last she very quietly excused herself.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter on his search for a second wife, 1613 (Brewster, The Martyrs of Science) &#183; tr. David Brewster, 1841 &#183; of a widow, the first of eleven women he considered; as David Brewster quotes him &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/25992/pg25992-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 539 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
